// This file begins with a UTF-8 byte-order mark, on purpose.
//
// Windows PowerShell writes one by default — `Set-Content -Encoding utf8`
// on 5.1 puts one at the head of every file it creates — so it is the first
// thing a Windows user meets, and the lexer used to read it as a stray
// character before any of the program was seen.
//
// The mark is zero width, so it is skipped and not consumed: the first
// real character of the next line is column 1, where an editor shows it.
// That the assertions below run at all is what this file asserts.
//
// KEEP THE MARK. An editor that strips it on save turns this back into an
// ordinary file, and the corpus stops asking the question.
val greeting = "a mark is not a character"
assert(greeting.length == 25)
assert(1 + 1 == 2)
